# options for PETSC using MPI
# To use his file, set environment variable PETSC_OPTIONS to
#       '-options_file  /path/to/this/file'

# Currently using GMRES Krylov space method as the main solver
# and algebraic multigrid as the preconditioner

# For more about the options, see
#    https://petsc.org/release/manualpages/KSP/KSPSetFromOptions
#    https://petsc.org/release/manualpages/PC/PCSetFromOptions/

# set min numbers of matrix rows per MPI rank  (default is 10000)
-mpi_linear_solver_server_minimum_count_per_rank 5000
-mpi_linear_solver_server
-mpi_linear_solver_server_view
-mpi_linear_solver_server_use_shared_memory false

# Krylov linear solver:
-ksp_type gmres
-ksp_max_it 200

# -ksp_reuse_preconditioner: now ON as an optimization.  It was OFF while we
# established the iteration-count floor for a fair comparison.  Now that
# fieldsplit is the winner, reuse amortizes the (expensive) redundant-LU
# factorization across timesteps: levels 2+ factor once per grid configuration
# and reuse the factors until the next regrid, instead of refactoring on every
# solve.  The code forces level 1 to always rebuild (petsc_driver.f90:116),
# which is fine since level 1 is cheap.
#   RISK: stale factors (matrix values change as h evolves) raise iteration
#   counts somewhat.  If a solve fails to converge within -ksp_max_it it becomes
#   a HARD ERROR (KSPSetErrorIfNotConverged is TRUE, petsc_driver.f90:108).  If
#   that happens, raise -ksp_max_it or comment this back out.
-ksp_reuse_preconditioner

# convergence criterion for linear solver (larger than PETSc default):
-ksp_rtol 1.e-9

# ===================== preconditioner =====================
# ---- ORIGINAL: algebraic multigrid (baseline in NOTES) ----
#-pc_type gamg
#-pc_type hypre  # requires installing hypre

# ---- fieldsplit: split the 2x2 blocks into u (x-eqns) and v (y-eqns) -------
# The matrix has block size 2 with unknowns interleaved u0,v0,u1,v1,...
# PCFIELDSPLIT with block size 2 makes  field 0 = u  and  field 1 = v.
#   I-D11 (u<->u) is tridiagonal in x, I-D22 (v<->v) is tridiagonal in y.
#   -D12,-D21 are the cross-derivative coupling between the fields.
# Each field block is a direct sum of independent 1D tridiagonals, so an EXACT
# block solve via redundant+LU is cheap and needs no MUMPS.
-pc_type fieldsplit
-pc_fieldsplit_block_size 2

# ---- coupling strategy between the u and v fields ----
# CURRENT: multiplicative = block Gauss-Seidel (solve u, update, solve v; outer
#   GMRES handles the residual coupling).  This is the WINNER (230s w/ reuse,
#   iters 3/5/7.6/10/12) because it keeps BOTH blocks as cheap independent 1D
#   tridiagonals.
-pc_fieldsplit_type multiplicative
# REJECTED: Schur complement.  Tested and it was ~30x SLOWER (6619s) despite
#   slightly fewer iterations (2.2/3.4/6.3/8.8/11.0).  Reason: selfp forms
#   Sp = A11 - A10 inv(diag A00) A01, and the A10/A01 cross-coupling makes Sp a
#   wide-band 2D-coupled operator (bandwidth ~nx), NOT tridiagonal.  redundant+lu
#   on that has ~N*nx fill, replicated per rank -> per-iteration cost explodes.
#   The whole advantage here is keeping the blocks as 1D tridiagonals; Schur
#   destroys exactly that.  Do not re-enable.
#-pc_fieldsplit_type schur
#-pc_fieldsplit_schur_fact_type full
#-pc_fieldsplit_schur_precondition selfp

# ---- how each field block is solved (shared by schur and multiplicative) ----
# EXACT via redundant+lu: correct at any rank count, but replicates the block on
#   every rank and factors SERIALLY (does not parallelize; wall ~= cpu).  With
#   -ksp_reuse_preconditioner ON the factorization is amortized across timesteps.
# field 0 = u  (x-direction tridiagonal block)
-fieldsplit_0_ksp_type preonly
-fieldsplit_0_pc_type redundant
-fieldsplit_0_redundant_pc_type lu
# field 1 = v: under schur this KSP solves the (approximate) Schur complement,
#   not A11 -- redundant+lu handles either sparsity pattern.
-fieldsplit_1_ksp_type preonly
-fieldsplit_1_pc_type redundant
-fieldsplit_1_redundant_pc_type lu

# ---- FUTURE (needs MUMPS linked): distributed LU that actually parallelizes.
#      Replace the two redundant blocks above with these:
#-fieldsplit_0_pc_type lu
#-fieldsplit_0_pc_factor_mat_solver_type mumps
#-fieldsplit_1_pc_type lu
#-fieldsplit_1_pc_factor_mat_solver_type mumps
# ==========================================================


# debug options:
#-fp_trap off
# NOTE: -log_view appears to crash at finalize under -mpi_linear_solver_server,
# so leave it OFF.  The code's own "Linear solve time" report gives wall/cpu
# timing already, which is enough for comparing preconditioners.
#-log_view
#-start_in_debugger -debugger_rank 0
#-ksp_monitor
#-ksp_view
#-info

# print options at end of run:
-options_view

# test if any options are not used:
# (leave ON while experimenting with fieldsplit: any option listed as "not used"
#  means PETSc ignored it -- a typo or the mpi server didn't honor it)
-options_left
